WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.882 --> 00:00:09.092
That Navy SEAL you see

00:00:09.092 --> 00:00:12.095
before you is holding
a gun made by Biofire.

00:00:13.263 --> 00:00:16.391
Its very, very big claim
is that it's the first company

00:00:16.391 --> 00:00:18.768 position:63%
to make a smart gun that actually works.

00:00:19.728 --> 00:00:20.895
That's right.

00:00:21.187 --> 00:00:24.399
James Bond-style
fingerprint and facial recognition.

00:00:25.692 --> 00:00:28.820
The gun only fires
if the right person is holding it.

00:00:29.404 --> 00:00:31.781 position:58%
It knows somebody is holding onto it.

00:00:31.781 --> 00:00:34.284 position:61%
But no matter how much he pulls
the trigger, nothing's going to happen.

00:00:43.334 --> 00:00:46.087
The person who started
Biofire is Kai Kloepfer.

00:00:46.838 --> 00:00:50.341 position:97%
He's all of 25 years old and has been working on making this gun

00:00:50.341 --> 00:00:55.305 position:97%
for ten years, a project he started shortly after the Aurora theater shooting.

00:00:56.014 --> 00:00:58.516 position:63%
At the time, it's
like the worst mass shooting in history.

00:00:59.893 --> 00:01:04.022 position:63%
And it happened right just down the road
here from where I grew up.

00:01:04.189 --> 00:01:07.400 position:64%
I was fortunate enough to not have
to think about gun violence or gun deaths

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:09.819
and that was really
my first sort of encounter

00:01:09.819 --> 00:01:10.653
with the topic, right?

00:01:10.653 --> 00:01:12.489
I was just starting to
grow up, just starting to think

00:01:12.489 --> 00:01:14.115
about these kind
of topics as a 15 year old

00:01:15.283 --> 00:01:16.284
and really start to think about:

00:01:16.284 --> 00:01:20.080 position:58%
how could we do something about this?

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.123 position:58%
I started thinking about this concept
of a smart gun.

00:01:22.123 --> 00:01:24.250 position:66%
By no means am I the first person to think
about this, obviously,

00:01:25.335 --> 00:01:27.670 position:66%
and it's been science fiction forever, but

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:30.298 position:59%
nobody had ever really built a working
smart gun.

00:01:31.049 --> 00:01:33.093
Okay, so you're 15. Yeah.

00:01:33.093 --> 00:01:36.346 position:56%
And instead of doing some policy
that you're kind of you're inspired.

00:01:36.638 --> 00:01:39.224 position:64%
Yeah. I was never a policy person, right?

00:01:39.224 --> 00:01:41.851 position:61%
I've been forced to become a little bit
more of one, you know, over the last

00:01:41.851 --> 00:01:43.186 position:56%
ten years. But no I was an engineer.

00:01:43.186 --> 00:01:45.438
I was a socially awkward nerd.

00:01:45.438 --> 00:01:49.651 position:94%
A bona fide nerd who clinched his nerd bona fides by getting

00:01:49.651 --> 00:01:55.198 position:97%
an award at the very prestigious Intel International Science and Engineering Fair.

00:01:55.573 --> 00:01:58.451 position:61%
So, okay, so for people who don't know,
I mean, this is like no joke.

00:01:58.451 --> 00:02:02.372 position:63%
It's like, yes, thousands of the world's
smartest kids

00:02:02.372 --> 00:02:05.750 position:64%
Top. The year I went, it was the top 1500
out of over 8 million people.

00:02:05.792 --> 00:02:07.085
8 million people. Right.

00:02:07.085 --> 00:02:09.129 position:59%
Yeah. Like this is the top of the top.

00:02:09.129 --> 00:02:11.172
It’s as high as it gets
I think for the science fair world.

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:14.676
At the time
I was mostly an electrical engineer

00:02:15.718 --> 00:02:17.679 position:56%
and I could sort of fake my way
through some mechanical engineering.

00:02:17.679 --> 00:02:20.640 position:72%
So built a pretty good electrical system and a
really s***** mechanical system

00:02:21.266 --> 00:02:23.768 position:56%
Sort of slapped them together
into this kind of gun-shaped object,

00:02:24.144 --> 00:02:25.395
not an actual firearm in any way,

00:02:25.395 --> 00:02:27.397 position:58%
I wasn't allowed to bring a gun
to the science fair, quite obviously.

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:30.733
I was very surprised by all this.

00:02:30.733 --> 00:02:33.695
You know, I'd done a lot of science
fair projects before.

00:02:33.695 --> 00:02:36.156 position:63%
Three quarters of the time people didn't
believe that I'd actually built them.

00:02:36.156 --> 00:02:37.740 position:58%
They thought my parents had did them.

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:40.118
I mean this in the nicest way
possible, Dad, I'm sorry, but

00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:43.955 position:67%
my dad needs help like turning his iPad on.

00:02:44.581 --> 00:02:49.294
After ten years of toil, Kai
is understandably proud

00:02:49.294 --> 00:02:51.963 position:61%
that his business finally has a product
to sell.

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:54.674 position:66%
After you. Today we've got one of our Mark

00:02:54.674 --> 00:02:58.094
Mark II prototypes, production
units of the smart gun.

00:02:58.094 --> 00:03:02.348 position:66%
So as soon as I start to interact with it,
I'm going to see those white lights.

00:03:03.016 --> 00:03:04.684
The white lights
basically mean that it's active.

00:03:04.684 --> 00:03:07.854 position:61%
It sees that somebody has picked it up,
but it hasn't recognized the user yet.

00:03:07.896 --> 00:03:09.397
So it's still locked.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:13.359 position:64%
To set up the gun, you register
your face and fingerprints on this device

00:03:13.359 --> 00:03:17.197 position:63%
in less than a minute where you can also
add and delete other users.

00:03:23.703 --> 00:03:28.166
If you're not registered, Nothing.

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:32.378 position:58%
Fingerprints are very well optimized,
very commercially mature.

00:03:32.378 --> 00:03:34.672 position:63%
You don't need a lot of processing power
to run them appropriately.

00:03:35.048 --> 00:03:36.966
Facial recognition
is a cutting edge biometric,

00:03:36.966 --> 00:03:39.844 position:66%
which means it requires
orders of magnitude more processing power.

00:03:39.844 --> 00:03:42.847 position:63%
So where previously we were able
to just use like little microcontrollers

00:03:42.847 --> 00:03:45.099 position:63%
and things like that to do fingerprints,
we've had to basically build

00:03:45.099 --> 00:03:48.811 position:66%
an entire computer hardened for the system
that works inside of a gun

00:03:48.937 --> 00:03:51.439 position:63%
to be able to run facial recognition
at the speeds that our customers demand.

00:03:54.442 --> 00:03:54.859
I’ve shot

00:03:54.859 --> 00:03:57.403
guns before,
but am far from a gun enthusiast.

00:03:58.279 --> 00:03:59.072
In many ways.

00:03:59.072 --> 00:04:01.491
I'm Biofire’s target audience.

00:04:01.491 --> 00:04:03.534 position:63%
A dad who wants to keep his family safe,

00:04:03.743 --> 00:04:08.206 position:66%
but who also thinks having a gun at home
probably ends up more dangerous than good.

00:04:09.374 --> 00:04:12.752
Still, old gun or new, I
